Month = {JUL},
Abstract = {This article presents a conceptual clarification of asymmetric
   hypotheses and a discussion of methodologies available to test them.
   Despite the existence of a litany of theories that posit asymmetric
   hypotheses, most empirical studies fail to capture their core insight:
   boundaries separating zones of data from areas that lack data are
   substantively interesting. We discuss existing set-theoretic and large-N
   approaches to the study of asymmetric hypotheses, introduce new ones
   from the literatures on stochastic frontier and data envelopment
   analysis, evaluate their relative merits, and give three examples of how
   asymmetric hypotheses can be studied with this suite of tools.},
